# Day 03 - Process Management

## Objective

Learn how Linux manages running programs and how to monitor, control, stop, resume, terminate, and change the priority of processes.

## Topics Covered

* Program vs Process
* Process ID (PID)
* Foreground and Background Processes
* Process Monitoring
* Process Signals
* Stop and Resume Processes
* Process Termination
* Nice Value and Process Priority
* `renice`

## Important Commands

| Command          | Purpose                                    |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `ps`             | Display processes                          |
| `ps aux`         | Display detailed process information       |
| `pgrep`          | Find a process PID                         |
| `top`            | Monitor processes in real time             |
| `htop`           | Interactive process monitoring             |
| `jobs`           | Display background jobs                    |
| `bg`             | Resume a stopped job in background         |
| `fg`             | Bring a job to foreground                  |
| `kill PID`       | Terminate a process normally               |
| `kill -9 PID`    | Forcefully terminate a process             |
| `kill -STOP PID` | Stop/pause a process                       |
| `kill -CONT PID` | Resume a stopped process                   |
| `nice`           | Start a process with a specific nice value |
| `renice`         | Change the nice value of a running process |

## Process Priority

Linux uses a **nice value** to influence CPU scheduling priority.

```text
-20  → Highest priority
  0  → Normal priority
+19  → Lowest priority
```

Remember:

> Lower nice value = Higher priority

> Higher nice value = Lower priority

## Example

```bash
sleep 300 &
pgrep sleep
ps -o pid,ni,comm -p PID
renice -n 10 -p PID
```

## Learning Outcome

After completing this day, I understand how to:

* Identify running processes
* Find process IDs
* Monitor CPU and memory usage
* Run processes in foreground and background
* Stop and resume processes
* Terminate processes using signals
* Understand Linux process signals
* Change process scheduling priority using `renice`

